// linenoise as C declares it.
//
// **Everything that is C lives in this directory** — `linenoise.c`, `linenoise.h` as they come from
// upstream, and this file of declarations over them. Nothing here is meant to be called by a program:
// `linenoise` hands back `malloc`'d storage the caller must return, a boolean is an `int` under one of
// two opposite conventions, and a string is a pointer somebody else owns. The module above this one
// turns those into `Option`s and `bool`s and is what an application imports.
//
// The split keeps two jobs apart. This one has to be **faithful** — a signature that disagrees with
// the header links perfectly and corrupts the call at run time, and nothing in this arrangement checks
// one against the other, so each declaration below was read out of `linenoise.h` rather than
// remembered. The other one has to be **pleasant**, which is a different question and would otherwise
// be answered in the same breath.
//
// **There is no shim here at all, and no `c const`.** Every entry point linenoise publishes takes and
// returns scalars and pointers, so declaring it is the whole of reaching it; and the library has no
// `#define` a caller ever needs — the two mode switches are separate functions rather than flags.
// `15 §7`'s three C-only shapes simply do not arise, which is why this is the smallest raw layer in the
// organisation.
//
// **Nor is there anything for `impl Drop`**, and that is worth stating because a binding to a library
// that allocates usually owes one. The only allocation linenoise makes is the line it just read, and it
// has to be freed before the string is even inspected — see `read` above. There is no handle, no
// session and no editor object, so there is nothing whose lifetime a destructor could be attached to.

module sh.sysl.linenoise.c

// Read one line. **Answers storage `malloc`'d by linenoise**, which the caller must hand back with
// `free` below, and `null` at end of input.
extern "linenoise" linenoise(prompt: *u8) -> *u8

// `linenoiseFree`, which is the library's own `free` rather than the C library's — the point of it
// being published is that a program need not have been linked against the same allocator.
extern "linenoiseFree" free(p: *u8)

// ## History
//
// **These four use two opposite conventions, and it is linenoise's doing rather than a slip.** `add`
// and `set_max_len` answer 1 for success; `save` and `load` answer 0 for success and -1 for failure.
// The module above turns both into `bool` and says so where a reader will see it.

extern "linenoiseHistoryAdd" history_add(line: *u8) -> int
extern "linenoiseHistorySetMaxLen" history_set_max_len(len: int) -> int
extern "linenoiseHistorySave" history_save(path: *u8) -> int
extern "linenoiseHistoryLoad" history_load(path: *u8) -> int

// ## The terminal, and the two modes

extern "linenoiseClearScreen" clear_screen()
extern "linenoiseSetMultiLine" set_multiline(on: int)

// Mask mode hides what is typed, for reading a password. Two functions rather than one taking a flag,
// which is linenoise's shape.
extern "linenoiseMaskModeEnable" mask_enable()
extern "linenoiseMaskModeDisable" mask_disable()
